# A lambda with 2 or 3 parameters is COMPILED, not interpreted.
#
# The lift was gated on `nParams <= 1` because the bound-fn bridge was assumed
# to pass a single argument. It is not: pyboundfn_callvn carries a0/a1/a2 and
# places NOwn of them ahead of the bound slots, and pyboundfn_setown sets NOwn —
# the runtime was already parameterised in count, only this lowering was not. It
# reserved exactly one own slot, so a 2- or 3-parameter lambda stayed a pyeval
# SOURCE closure: right answers, ~7x the per-call cost.
#
# An output diff cannot see this fix — the answers were already correct. The
# test is `strings <binary>`: an interpreted lambda carries its own SOURCE TEXT
# in the binary (project_nilpy_every_lambda_is_an_interpreted_source_closure),
# a compiled one does not. The Makefile asserts that count is ZERO, which is
# what makes this a regression test rather than a restatement of the answers.
#
# NOT covered here, deliberately: four or more parameters, which still take the
# interpreted fallback AND segfault when called — pre-existing on pinned, filed
# as bug-nilpy-a-four-parameter-lambda-segfaults-when-called. Nor the
# two-iterable map(f, xs, ys), which the frontend does not parse at all.
#
# Every expectation is CPython's own output.
# bug-nilpy-multi-parameter-lambdas-are-still-interpreted

k = 10

f = lambda a, b: a + b + k          # two own params AND a capture
g = lambda a, b, c: a * b + c       # three
h = lambda x: x * 2                 # one — the case that already lifted
z = lambda: 42                      # none — lifted under a dummy own param


def use(fn):
    return fn(2, 5)


print(f(3, 4), g(2, 3, 4), h(5), z())
print(use(lambda a, b: a * b))      # passed as a value, called through a param
print(sorted([3, 1, 2], key=lambda v: -v))
